  "description": "A small, MIT-licensed .xlsx writer over DocumentFormat.OpenXml. Styles, number formats, data validation, tables, defined names, pictures, freeze panes and auto-fit — plus a streaming writer for exports too large to hold in memory — with an API shaped like the one you already know from EPPlus, and no commercial licence.",
  "id": "Sheetwright",
  "isPrerelease": false,
  "lastEdited": "2026-10-01T23:06:04.773Z",
  "licenseExpression": "MIT",
  "licenseUrl": "https://licenses.nuget.org/MIT",
  "listed": true,
  "packageHash": "jv47NSmeH6HdYP54sf4kYqdd0g5aKKlBBYr+v5Ct/3mqVpxGkhib8NY/AMCC4GFosvTi4KXFtkuacdnIsrWJbw==",
  "packageHashAlgorithm": "SHA512",
  "packageSize": 197537,
  "projectUrl": "https://github.com/sinanoran/sheetwright",
  "published": "2026-10-01T23:02:57.417Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Added: SpreadsheetStreamWriter, a single worksheet written straight to a stream one row at a time, for an export too large to hold in memory. SpreadsheetPackage keeps every cell until it is asked for the bytes, which is what lets styles merge and columns auto-fit, and what runs a worker out of memory on a report of several hundred thousand rows. The streaming writer keeps nothing but the row in hand, and the bytes reach the destination as the rows arrive. A table on a streamed sheet may be left open at the bottom — pass null for its last row and it ends at the last row written.\n\nFixed: NaN and the infinities are written as text rather than as numbers. The file format has no numeric spelling for any of the three, so a numeric cell holding NaN produced a workbook Excel offered to repair rather than open.\n\nChanged: a streamed table and a table on an in-memory sheet resolve their column names through the same code, so an empty heading becomes its column letter and a repeated one gains a suffix identically in both.\n\nFull notes: https://github.com/sinanoran/sheetwright/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md",
